I suggest a small "instruct" model with about 3 to 4 billion parameters from the Qwen, Llama, or Gemma families, and try two or three of them before committing. 
Which exact version matters less than the training data. The search results I found were mixed and partly out of date, so check Hugging Face for the newest small version of each family.

## Why a small model

Many teams now pick models in the 1 to 7 billion parameter range and fine-tune them for one job. 
Once specialized, these compact models can match or beat older models of 20B+ parameters. 
Small models are also easier to run cheaply, and that fits your budget and your student pilot.

source: https://articles.firstaimovers.com/articles/fine-tuning-llms-vs-rag-2026/

A small model also fits free tools. 
A model of about 3 to 4B parameters can usually be fine-tuned on a free Colab GPU using a memory-saving method called **QLoRA**. 
Big models (27B and up) won't fit, and you don't need them for short reading hints.

## Models to try first

| Family    | Good size to try                    | Why                                                               |
|-----------|-------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------|
| **Qwen**  | about 3-4B                          | Follows instructions well, and has many tutorials for fine-tuning |
| **Llama** | 3.2 3B (or 1B if your GPU is small) | Very popular, so lots of guides and help online                   |
| **Gemma** | 4B (or 1B)                          | Small and easy to run                                             |

If we jusg want one to start with, take the **Qwen ~3-4B instruct** or **Llama 3.2 3B instruct** version. 
Always pick the version whose name ends in **"instruct"**, because those already know how to follow directions. 
(An instruct model starts as a base model, 
then gets extra training on examples of people asking for something and getting a helpful answer. 
It learns that a message is a request and that it should respond to it. 
That's why instruct models work well right away, and why they work with chat-style formats. 
Base models generally don't handle those chat formats out of the box.)

Check the license too. Most of these are fine for a thesis, but read the terms of the one you choose and note it in your documentation.

## How to choose between them

Test them yourself:

1. Write about 20 example situations, such as a sentence a student is stuck on and the kind of hint you'd want.
2. Give the same 20 to each model, **before any training**.
3. Have your partner teacher (or your group) score the hints.
4. Pick the best one. Those scores are also your **"before" numbers**, which your fine-tuned model has to beat.

If we have Filipino texts, include a few in the 20 examples, since models differ in how well they handle Filipino.

## T5?

T5 works well for generating quiz questions, but it's weaker at writing friendly hints. 
For a beginner, **one model for everything** is easier: the same fine-tuned model writes hints and quiz questions. 
We can add T5 later if question quality is a problem.

## What matters more than the model

The **training data**. A small model trained on a few hundred good, 
teacher-checked examples beats a bigger model trained on messy ones. 
The panel will also ask how you made your dataset, more than which model you picked. 
That's why the labeling guide and the teacher-labeled samples in your plan are so important.

## Suggested tools

- **Unsloth** or **Hugging Face (transformers + peft)** for training. Both have beginner notebooks for Colab.
- Start with about 200 to 500 examples for a first test run, just to check that the whole process works before you collect more.
